Washington, DCSenator Orrin Hatch (R-UT),thesenior Republicanand President Pro Tempore of the US Senate, released the following statement after his historic lands bill,theEmery County Public Land Management Act of 2018,passed markup in the Senate Committee on Energy and Natural Resources. The product of two decades of outreach and negotiation,this landmarkbill will protect over one million acres in Utah's Emery County, establishinga new model for future collaborative land conservation efforts.
I am grateful to see the Emery County Public Lands Management Act pass without objection in the Senate Energy and Natural Resources Committee, setting up a path forward for passage into law later this year,said Hatch.This bill reflects the input of over 25 stakeholder groups, local and state leaders, diverse recreation groups, access advocates, paleontologists, and the conservation community. This historic proposal will offer unprecedented conservation in Utah. It will also bring much-needed management clarity for folks on the ground who depend on these lands.
The legislation has received widespread support from groups and organizations spanning the political spectrum.Click here to read more. To read the Daily Herald op-ed that Senator Hatch and Representative John Curtis (R-UT 3) wrote together,click here.
